\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-25}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}

\p{\strong{State.} On the evening of 2026-11-24 [the machine became the default engine](log-65Z0) with [the default switch set](dec-YP0N), the release became [one executable](design-packaging), and the set of jars was frozen. The items below were known at that point and are not in the release: some were measured and kept out, some were bounded and not explained, some were never started. Each names what is known and what would close it. Source: [the finalization session](ses-A14B). Revised on 2026-11-25 after the two optimisation waves of that day, [the first](dec-JT59) and [the second](dec-RHRE), and the level fix: an item says where they changed it, and the section after the tolerated rows has what they opened. Revised again on 2026-11-25 for the maintainer's instruction of that day that the machine is to be the whole checker in production: the last section is the work list of that alignment.}

\subtree{
\title{Bounded, not explained}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The root cause of finding INT7-F1.} With \code{eager}, a probe of the plain phase evaluated one unit of con-leche for 28.6 M ticks into the limit of a session; [a tick budget](log-P6FV) of 2^22 now cuts it and the unit goes to the reference engine. Known: the probe is the Nat folding of an open pair, it evaluates the arguments of an open term, and the official plain procedure never starts that evaluation; the largest probe that completes on the eight corpora needs 2.75 M ticks; the switch still costs con-leche 2.2 \% against the set without it (1.257 s against 1.230 s, JVM launcher, warm, 8 workers). Not known: why that probe is so large, and whether a probe can be recognised as hopeless before it is paid for. What would close it: the unit decided by the machine, or the probe not started, with the gate of \code{eager} unchanged. \strong{Narrowed on 2026-11-25, not closed:} [the default budget is 2^17 ticks](log-I3WM), in a gap of the probe sizes, so this unit costs the machine 133,229 ticks instead of 4,196,461 before it is declined, and con-leche needs 5.74 \% fewer ticks (one worker); the four units of cslib and Mathlib whose probes completed after up to 2.75 M ticks are cut as well and go to the reference engine, and the largest probe that still completes has 63,654 ticks. Why the probe of the con-leche unit is so large is as unknown as before. See [the report](rep-OPT-I), section 2.}
\li{\strong{The 2 percent of the shortcuts on reachability.} The default set is 1.7 \% slower than the set with \code{eager} alone on reachability in a window of six rounds and 1.8 \% in a window of eight; \code{congruence} costs it 2.6 \% against the final set without it, and \code{skipargs} on top of \code{congruence} is the borderline cell of [the decision](dec-YP0N), +3.7 \% by the median and +2.1 \% by the mean. On the native image the two sets are equal there, 0.545 s. Not known: where the time goes on this corpus of closed evaluation; the tables have only the times. What would close it: a profile of that pass, and a threshold or a cheaper derivation if the cost is real.}
\li{\strong{The gap in the argument for eager reduction}, carried over from [the closed problem of the switch](prob-8MJ2): a reduction by the K rule whose plain and eager computation the machine both skips because its two operands are one value. No export for it could be produced and it cannot be excluded.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{Measured and kept out}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The pooled session arrays with the young reserve of finding J1-F1}: correct by its gates, and [3.7 percent slower on Mathlib with more resident memory](ins-JJ7I) on the default set. Not separated: which of the two parts costs. The reserve belongs to a decision about the heap line of the JVM launcher, where a heap of 6 GB for the machine waits for it. Neither wave of 2026-11-25 measured it again; the second one changed the tables of a session, not its slabs, see [warm sessions](design-warm-sessions).}
\li{\strong{The layout of the level stores.} A memo in front of universe instantiation [does not pay](ins-258N): the cost is the first touch, in each session, of level data that every worker holds in its own copy. What the census points at is one shared base instead of a copy per worker, and records instead of parallel arrays. That layout was not prototyped at the release. \strong{Since 2026-11-25 the first half is in the default build:} [the workers share the frozen level base](log-ZMAE), which took 0.40 s off the executable's Mathlib pass and 3 GiB off its resident set, see [the report](rep-OPT-C). Still open after the second wave: records instead of parallel arrays for the level nodes and the cache entries, and the loader's own copy of the level store per publication, which is 8.7 \% of the page faults of the loader thread in a Mathlib pass of the executable, see [the report](rep-OPT-F), section 3.}
\li{The visit filter on the tabling keys of evaluation was measured again on the set of wave 6 and is a no-go there, see [the report](rep-O1); it is closed, not open, and is listed here because it had been in the same queue.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{Not started}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Identity on demand}, the switch \code{lazyid} of [the decision](dec-36MX). Known: the seven patches of the prototype apply to the main tree of the evening without a failed hunk. Not done: the named switch that stays off (the modes are bits of a system property today), the proof that the off state costs nothing (one test per key-forming call and per application built; it needs a comparison on the eight corpora under the guard of 3 \%), and the gates with the switch on. The open question of the prototype itself is unchanged: [no gain at eight workers in the native image](prob-BHBU).}
\li{\strong{x86-64.} Every timing and every instruction count of the project is from the aarch64 development host; the arena's runner is x86-64. The native image and the release workflow never ran on x86-64. The pin of the toolchain for it was compared with the published checksum; the archive was not downloaded on the development host. The module tests run on the hosted runner of the continuous integration, with that toolchain, and pass there; nothing was timed there. The two waves of 2026-11-25 changed nothing of this: all their measurements are from the aarch64 development host. Not established for the optional profile-guided build: whether a profile recorded on aarch64 serves an x86-64 build. See also [the projected board places](ins-G5UB).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{Settled by review, not proven}

\p{\strong{The list of tolerated rows.} At the release the hostile suite had 1,196 rows with official verdicts; 83 carried the mark of the tolerated kind, and through the machine-default launcher 68 of them were accepted against the official rejection. Since 2026-11-25 it has 1,274 rows, 94 with the mark and 78 accepted, see [the changelog entry](log-T6DI); since the default configuration takes a rejection of the machine as final, later that day, 74 are accepted, because four of the 78 had been accepts of the re-check's strict machine and now get the official rejection. That every one of these statements is derivable is what makes the accepts tolerable under [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract). It rests on four kinds of evidence, none of them a proof:}
\ul{
\li{certificates, a proof of the same statement that the official checker accepts: 15 rows of the shortcuts' review one by one, and certificates or accepted twins for the classes of stand-in types and of cache order, as the reviews that found them record;}
\li{a family argument without a certificate per row: the 19 rows of one family under eager reduction;}
\li{the integrator's own use of a reviewer's family argument for four rows, published with the request to object, and for the two twins without a node of [the metadata finding](ex-FD6K);}
\li{for the 11 marked rows added on 2026-11-25, a certificate each that the official checker accepts, named in the manifest; the marks were given by the package that wrote the rows, not by a reviewer, see [the report](rep-OPT-H), section 5;}
\li{for the one hostile file that is accepted only since a repeat of an accepted declaration is not checked again, that the statement of a repeat is the statement of its original, see [the accept of a repeat](design-verdict-protocol-repeats);}
\li{the argument of [the divergence note](design-official-divergence) that every step of the machine is a conversion that holds in the theory. Nothing of it is mechanised.}
}
\p{What would close it: a certificate accepted by the official checker for every row of the kind, kept beside the row; or a mechanised statement of the argument. As it is, the manifest of the suite names for every marked row who gave it the kind and on which ground.}
}

\subtree{
\title{Opened by the two waves of 2026-11-25}

\ul{
\li{\strong{One id space for level lists and name lists.} [The level fix](log-MVGK) made the instantiation caches and the term store ask the content of a list where they had asked its id. The assumption behind the defect, that a base list id is a list of base levels, was made in four places, and as long as one store holds level lists, lists of parameter names, name sets and substitution pairs it can be made again, and each site has to defend itself by content. Proposed and not built: separate stores, or one bit of the id for the kind of list. Also not established: whether a holder other than the caches keeps an id across a reset in which it is issued again (an instrument that never reissues an id was planned and not built), and whether a hostile export can turn the defect of before the fix into a wrong accept; none was built. See [the report](rep-INV-1), section 7, and [universe levels](design-universe-levels-overlays).}
\li{\strong{The closed-pair table without a gain in time.} A table of comparison results for closed values that survives the machine's sessions, the switch \code{closedpairs}, removes 4.5 to 15.1 \% of the machine's ticks with 8 workers and gives no time that the guard resolves on navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib (CPU of the pass 0.996, 1.002 and 0.993 of the base's; the executable, three interleaved rounds). It is not in the main tree. Not known: where the time of a lookup goes; no profile was taken. What would close it: a gain in time on the large corpora, and then the adversarial pass by an agent that does not own the code, which [the rule for a default](dec-QN1O) requires and which was not made. See [the report](rep-OPT-H), sections 7.4 and 8.}
\li{\strong{Huge pages for the executable's heap.} Advice for the image's large mappings is worth 2 to 14 \% of a warm pass while the kernel has free blocks of 2 MiB, and costs 10 to 50 \% when it has none, because an advised first touch then waits for the kernel to compact memory. The mechanism, a wrapper around the image's \code{mmap}, was not adopted; the JVM launcher's \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseTransparentHugePages~} has the same exposure. What would close it: a guard for an advice that watches the kernel's free blocks, taken in both states of a machine's memory, and a measurement on a machine short of memory such as the arena's. See [the report](rep-OPT-G), sections 6, 7 and 13.}
\li{\strong{A tick in a large session is still dearer.} The young and old session tables of the second wave are a gain at the cap of 1 MiB and did not make a larger cap pay, see [the cost of one machine operation](prob-28KQ).}
\li{\strong{The remaining distance to gruszka.} After the two waves, warm pass with 8 workers on a quiet host and gruszka with its arena run line in the same windows: the executable needs 1.05, 1.12, 1.19, 1.26, 1.06 and 1.14 times gruszka's wall clock on Init, Std, con-leche, navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib (12.29 against 10.77 s), is level on cedar (0.99) and far ahead on reachability (0.21); the JVM launcher is ahead on six corpora (Mathlib 0.96) and behind on con-leche (1.02) and navier-stokes-euler (1.13). By CPU both are behind on seven corpora, the executable by 24 to 63 \% and the JVM launcher by 8 to 44 \%, and by the arena's metric, instructions, the executable needs 3.0 to 3.5 times gruszka's count. Known and not closed: the native image is dearer than the JVM in the workers' own code, mostly the code of every array access ([rep-OPT-A](rep-OPT-A), section 7); navier-stokes-euler is the largest distance on both launchers; and what is left was not profiled after the second wave. What would close it: the executable at gruszka's wall clock on Mathlib, under the guard. See [the integration of the second wave](rep-INT9), section 7, and [the gap to gruszka](prob-TJK3).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{The work list of the alignment: what the machine must do before it is the whole checker}

\p{Opened on 2026-11-25. The maintainer's instruction of that day, recorded in [standing policies](standing-policies-conduct), is that the default configuration gives its verdict from the machine alone, that the machine is aligned with the official checker, and that the reference engine is a tool of development. The first step is done: a rejection of the machine is final and the launcher makes no second run, see [the decision](dec-PUU0). This section is what is left, counted by [the census of package MO-1](rep-MO-1), sections 2 to 6, on the build before that step; the machine's jar is the same in the build after it. Nothing in it is a timing of that day.}

\p{\strong{1. What a plain run still asks of the reference engine.} The first two rows are the decline fallback, which \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~} turns off; with it off the machine alone accepts Init and reachability and declines the other six corpora at their first such unit. The third is asked in every configuration.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Use}\th{On real input}\th{On hand-made input}\th{What the machine would have to do}\th{State}}
\tr{\td{The K rule after the application of a let variable whose declared type is not the type of its value, finding E1-F6: the machine declines by a rule}\td{86 units, 75 different declarations: Std 1, cedar 1, con-leche 1, navier-stokes-euler 16, cslib 7, Mathlib 60}\td{3 hostile rows}\td{Type the major premise of the K rule as the official kernel does, from the unreduced term. The machine's value of such an application has the let variable reduced away, so the type of the value can be a stand-in that is only convertible with the official one, see [the stand-in types](design-official-divergence-standin). The value has to keep the let variable, or its declared type, to where the K rule asks.}\td{A package, MO-2, is working on it. Nothing is published.}}
\tr{\td{A probe of the plain phase of eager reduction that is cut at its budget}\td{9 units, 5 different declarations: con-leche 1, cslib 4, Mathlib 4}\td{none; one hostile row is declined for the same question inside the comparison of the K rule}\td{Decide the probe: learn whether the plain and the eager mode agree at that point without evaluating what the official plain procedure never starts. Why the probe of the con-leche unit is so large is the first item of this note.}\td{A package, MO-3, is working on it. Nothing is published.}}
\tr{\td{The environment pass: every inductive block and the quotient package are admitted with judgments of the reference engine}\td{17,409 units of the eight corpora, 1.1 \% of all units, 7,133 of them Mathlib's}\td{every file with an inductive type}\td{Admission asking its judgments of the machine. It asks them through one interface, which is where that would be done, see [admission](design-inductive-admission). How much the answers of the two engines can differ there was not examined.}\td{Not started. No option touches it.}}
}

\p{Two more classes of declines exist on small inputs and block nothing real: two mutants that the machine declines for memory and the reference engine rejects within the limit, not analysed; and the two arena tests on which the official kernel does not terminate, where a decline is the answer of both engines.}

\p{\strong{2. Rejections of what the official checker accepts.} The gate of the default configuration counts twelve, all on rows of the hostile suite, with a ceiling that may only go down; they are described in [accepted risks](accepted-risks-wrong-rejects).}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Eight through a memo of an earlier declaration in a warm session.} Closed by a session that keeps no comparison result, no checked type and no full reduction from one declaration to the next. The same change takes away 15 of the tolerated accepts below and every known dependence of a verdict on the schedule, 21 hostile rows, see [the insight](ins-RPOG).}
\li{\strong{Four through tables keyed by values with canonical variables.} The machine rejects them with every switch set. Only the official caches close them without a decline: keyed by terms, per declaration, with a fresh variable per binder instance. There is no design for that and no cost.}
\li{\strong{A third family, outside the suite: a let variable used after the end of its let.} Found on 2026-11-25 by the package that works on the first row of the table, on the build before the change of the default. The export \code{mo2-escape-var-ok} is accepted by the official checker of Lean 4.36.0 and rejected by the machine alone, with a type mismatch of the declaration; a plain run of that build accepted it through the re-check of the rejection. Its shape, with \code{d}, \code{h} and \code{intro} as in [the common root of the divergence classes](design-official-divergence-root): a constant \code{\verb~|W : forall (q : d h = 5), P (Eq.rec 7 q)~} and the theorem \code{\verb~|P 7 := let q : d h = 5 := r0; W q~} with \code{\verb~|r0 : d intro = 5~}. Officially the type of a let term is again a let, and once the inference of the let has returned, the reduction puts the value in the place of the variable, so the K rule types its major premise by the type of the value, \code{\verb~|d intro = 5~}, and fires. The machine's let variable keeps its declared type for good, which is the fix of finding E1-F1, also where the term is used outside the let, and the expected-type rule compares inside it. Siblings of the family are accepted by the machine of that build against the official rejection, derivable statements and so of the tolerated kind, among them \code{mo2-escape-var-bad}, \code{mo2-inside-then-escape-bad} and \code{mo2-app-alias-exact-bad}. Since a rejection of the machine is now final, the default configuration rejects \code{mo2-escape-var-ok} by its protocol; that was not run again on the published build, and the file is not a row of the suite, so the gate's count of twelve does not include it. What would close it: the files of the family as rows of the suite with their official verdicts and marks, and the machine typing a let variable after the end of its let as the official kernel does. The package's change for the first row of the table is not published, and what it does with this family is for its own report. One thing is on record from its making: a first form of the change rejected one further official accept that the published build declines, \code{mo2-escape-lambda-body-let-ok}, a let-bound lambda whose body applies another let variable with the K rule after both lets, and accepted its officially rejected twin; the package found that with its own rows and is turning the case into a decline.}
}

\p{\strong{3. The tolerated accepts, by mechanism, and what each mechanism is worth.} 74 rows of the hostile suite are accepted against the official rejection, each a derivable statement. The practical contract tolerates them and the instruction did not withdraw that; the table says what aligning each group would mean and cost, should the alignment be taken that far. A row is attributed by removing switches until the machine alone no longer accepts it. The last column is what the journal had measured for the mechanism before; none of it was measured again. The times of the shortcuts and of \code{eager} are those of [the decision on the default set](dec-YP0N): warm wall clock of a pass that reloads the file, 8 workers, JVM launcher, aarch64 development host, the sets interleaved in quiet windows on 2026-11-24, before the two waves of 2026-11-25; "geometric mean" is over the eight corpora.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Mechanism}\th{Rows}\th{Aligning means}\th{What the mechanism is worth, as measured}}
\tr{\td{The shortcut \code{congruence}, outside eager reduction}\td{16}\td{The official argument comparison instead of the same-head probe, closure congruence and proofs by type, see [the shortcuts](design-proof-comparison-shortcuts).}\td{With \code{skipargs} already on: the geometric mean of the eight warm passes is 1.720 s with it and 1.801 s without, 4.7 \% longer; navier-stokes-euler 3.22 against 4.04 s, Mathlib 12.72 against 13.39 s.}}
\tr{\td{\code{congruence} under eager reduction}\td{22: 13 that the machine rejects without the shortcut, 9 that it declines without it}\td{The same, inside a declaration that enters eager reduction. Without the switch \code{eager} the machine declines all 22 and the reference engine gives the official rejection.}\td{As the row above. \code{eager} itself: 1.980 s against 2.065 s for the set of the wave-6 integration, Mathlib 18.02 against 20.49 s.}}
\tr{\td{The shortcut \code{skipargs}}\td{4}\td{Compare proof positions and the positions a definition does not read.}\td{With \code{congruence} already on: 1.720 s with it, 1.758 s without, 2.2 \% longer; navier-stokes-euler 3.22 against 3.79 s.}}
\tr{\td{Either shortcut}\td{3}\td{Both off.}\td{Both shortcuts together: 1.720 s against 1.980 s, the set without them is 15.1 \% longer; Mathlib 12.72 against 18.02 s, navier-stokes-euler 3.22 against 6.08 s, cslib 4.56 against 5.27 s.}}
\tr{\td{A memo that survives a declaration: \code{warm}}\td{11}\td{No comparison result, checked type or full reduction kept across declarations: the change that closes 8 of the wrong rejects.}\td{One worker, native image, the switches \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, median CPU time of three rounds, warm sessions at the better of the caps of 2 and 4 MiB against no warm session: Init 7.71 against 8.13 s, Std 13.80 against 13.72 s, the Cedar slice 3.32 against 3.40 s, see [the session cap](dec-KXE1). Not measured with the present default set, and not for a session that keeps only values.}}
\tr{\td{\code{pchk} or \code{warm}: the shared check facts}\td{4}\td{Neither; each alone still accepts the rows.}\td{\code{pchk}: 1.5 to 1.8 \% fewer instructions on Init and Std alone, 3.7 and 4.3 \% with \code{match} and \code{joint}, see [the switch reference](design-switches-document). Instructions, not time.}}
\tr{\td{The tabling gate \code{gated}}\td{1}\td{The raw major premise of the K rule typed as a term, finding E1-F7.}\td{No time of its own in the journal: without it every node is tabled, and a nest of 4,000 unshared levels costs 8,038,031 ticks instead of 60,017.}}
\tr{\td{No switch: the machine on values}\td{9}\td{The official type of an unreduced term, and the official caches keyed by terms.}\td{Not a switch, so nothing to turn off; the cost of an exact treatment was never measured.}}
\tr{\td{No switch: both engines}\td{4}\td{The loader and admission, not the machine: the reference engine accepts them under the practical policy too.}\td{Nothing in time.}}
}

\p{Outside the suite, the two arena tests of the class \code{either} that the default configuration accepts against the official rejection need the two shortcuts, and the one hostile file with a repeated theorem is accepted through the repeat skip, which is worth 1.4 \% of the geometric mean in the executable and 0.7 \% on the JVM launcher. Read as a work list: 45 of the 74 rows, and both arena tests, are the two proof-comparison shortcuts, which are also by far the largest single gain of the default set, 15 \% of the geometric mean and 42 \% of Mathlib when the set was chosen; 15 rows and 8 of the 12 wrong rejects are the memos of a warm session, which gain little where they were measured; 13 rows and 4 wrong rejects need the official caches and types, for which no design exists.}

\p{\strong{What would close it.} The eight corpora accepted with \code{\verb~|-X fallback=none~} and that setting made the default; the ceiling of the known wrong rejects at 0; admission by the machine; and the gate of the default configuration otherwise unchanged, with no accept of a statement that is not derivable. \strong{Not established:} anything on x86-64; whether further hand-made files of these families exist beyond the suite, which was not extended; the cost of every item for which the table says so.}
}

\p{See also: [the gap to gruszka on Mathlib](prob-TJK3), [the strict mode and object identity](prob-1YXM), [the cost of one machine operation](prob-28KQ).}
